WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:07.469
Once your project is ready, you can output media to disk and share images and videos with your audience.

2
00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.009
Open the file named 3-Media that's been provided to you.

3
00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:18.039
Start by setting the time of day to around 4.30pm. We'll change that again later if we need to.

4
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:27.719
Also unhide the animated people and the grass using the Scene Graph.

5
00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:48.880
Go to the Media panel in the dock. Here, you can define still images, videos, and panoramas to export.

6
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.960
There are also two other options that we'll discuss in a moment.

7
00:00:53.659 --> 00:01:03.060
To define a still image, the process is easy. Adjust the view to a shot you like, make sure Image Mode is active, and click the plus sign.

8
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:08.140
This creates a snapshot that you can now edit independently from the editor.

9
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:24.079
You can rename the view, but more importantly, you can adjust it in terms of time of day, exposure, weather, all of which would be parameters affecting this image and this image alone.

10
00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:31.349
A simple example would be to have a shot in daytime and duplicate the image to get a shot at nighttime.

11
00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:42.700
You can now switch back and forth between the two.

12
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:52.109
Another example may be to view the scene in a different season, by duplicating an image and changing the appropriate parameters.

13
00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:07.390
If you change your point of view, you will need to use the Refresh icon to update the image.

14
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.219
For some shots, you may decide to use a depth of field camera effect.

15
00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:58.469
Other interesting applications may lead you to use special filters for certain images.

16
00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:16.990
At any time, you can quit Media Mode to go back to regular editing.

17
00:03:18.669 --> 00:03:22.810
Another type of media that you can use is a 360-degree panorama.

18
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:34.030
Much like with still images, you position your camera where you need it to be, I'll use the Caffe, and then go to the Media Panorama panel and use the plus sign.

19
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:40.500
You get the same options you usually get with images, including camera effects and filters.

20
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.860
The 360 panorama will be created once you save it to disk, which we'll do in a moment.

21
00:03:48.039 --> 00:03:51.039
Next up are videos. Go to that panel.

22
00:03:53.659 --> 00:03:58.819
First, position your camera where the video will start. We'll animate the view to go around the building.

23
00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:04.490
The current camera position will be recorded as the first frame when you click the plus sign.

24
00:04:05.789 --> 00:04:13.289
Note that the video length is set by default to 10 seconds. We'll expand that to 30 to give ourselves enough time around the building.

25
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:25.079
To animate the camera, move your view to another point, and then click the plus sign on the right side of the clip to create a new keyframe.

26
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.259
Keep on moving. Move and adjust the camera position and create additional keyframes as needed.

27
00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.660
At the end, duplicate the last keyframe to just let the camera settle in its position.

28
00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:56.069
Ultimately, each sub-clip can be set to its own time of day to animate that effect if you so choose.

29
00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.899
Scrub the animation to see the results.

30
00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:38.480
Go back to the main video menu and rename the clip and then exit media mode.

31
00:05:39.529 --> 00:05:46.829
Now that you have defined your media, it is time to output it to disk. This is done by using the Export panel on the dock.

32
00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:55.629
Here you can choose which media to export. For example, under Images, you'll find the still images you have defined.

33
00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:10.990
You can choose the ones you want to render. You can do the same with Panoramas and with videos.

34
00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.860
There are some restrictions when using the community version of Twinmotion.

35
00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:21.060
Images and videos are saved to HD format and will have a watermark on them.

36
00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:28.379
Panoramas can be rendered as high as 6K in resolution. That's a small sacrifice to pay for free software.

37
00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:32.899
When you are ready to proceed, click the Start Export button.

38
00:06:34.139 --> 00:06:38.240
Choose or create an output folder and let the system process the data.

39
00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:46.550
This may take a while, especially when processing videos, so you may want to take a little break.

40
00:06:48.269 --> 00:06:53.670
Once the process is over, then you can view your files or share them with friends and clients.

41
00:07:08.339 --> 00:07:14.540
Another way of sharing media is through Presenter. In the Media panel, choose Presentation.

42
00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:21.069
Here you can create a presentation that includes some or all of the media you've already defined.

43
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:28.170
You can simply drag media and organize it on a timeline and you can mix between types.

44
00:07:28.569 --> 00:07:33.069
I think I'll use images and videos only for this and leave the panorama out.

45
00:07:35.029 --> 00:07:39.129
Once you have defined your presentation, you can go again to the Export panel.

46
00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:51.060
You can blank out the previous outputs since they're already saved and include the presentation file you have just defined.

47
00:07:55.879 --> 00:08:00.980
If you export it, again be aware that it might take a while to render, but also...

48
00:08:01.829 --> 00:08:08.529
it will create a self-executable that allows others to interact with your project without having Twinmotion installed.

49
00:08:09.579 --> 00:08:13.930
Because of that fact, Presenter saves more than just media.

50
00:08:14.329 --> 00:08:21.629
It compiles everything it needs to run the self-executable and therefore, presentation files tend to be large on the hard drives.

51
00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:25.410
The end result would look like this.

52
00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.879
And know that you can totally walk around and interact with your scene.

53
00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:42.870
Just select any image that you like and start walking around.

54
00:08:50.919 --> 00:08:57.919
This leaves one additional media option to learn about, working with phases, coming up in the next and last video.
